review.verdict:
Lens IB (intake verifier, supabase/verification/research_health_quick_order_local.mjs, diff 25858ad..121d0987): CORRECTIONS REQUIRED. I found 5 P2 findings (stage S2) and 10 P3 findings. I found no P0 and no P1.

What holds up:
- The verifier fails closed. It refuses while the receipt says sourceAcceptance PENDING and while the accepted currentness bytes still contain expected_columns:=null.
- It runs only in an owned PostgreSQL 17.x disposable with network none and tmpfs storage, and it rejects hosted targets.
- Fixtures are synthetic: example.invalid addresses, +10000000000, labels marked synthetic. Request rows in the new section come from the actual commit, not from direct inserts.
- BINDINGS maps correctly to the shared record: same path, doc71-disabled-source-bindings-v1, review 59940dc, the same 19 triggers, and 55 inventory entries.
- Every new SQL control expects the exact SQLSTATE, message and quickOrderCode detail found in the intake SQL at 121d0987.
- Several controls would fail if their behaviour were removed, each with a twin that differs in one value:
  - single save: five effects +1, readback from a second backend, same instant, enqueue-only, no reservation;
  - sequential payload conflict, and same-key replay or recovery (including after revocation and with an invalid digest);
  - a lock fault at each of the five writes, including the fifth (which shows four prior writes roll back);
  - member status, customer status, session revoked, EA actor mismatch, and the row-11 concurrent revoke;
  - the price-audience allowlist, member grant and decoy cases;
  - stock: an empty lot and a failed quality test each find no witness, and nine units are requested against two with no write;
  - the try-lock with an exclusive holder, including retry after the holder aborts, and the reentrant refusal;
  - IC-2 zero and partial offerings: marked refusal, no effects, key not consumed.

What needs correcting:
- The "concurrent" same-key and conflict cases (TX03) do not force the two calls to overlap.
- Two of the five try-lock (CC-18) controls required by doc 75 are absent.
- Readback of an actual commit checks only requestId, status and estimate.
- Setting refusals (Doc78 C-2) have only two controls.
- Most eligibility and trusted-context checks have no negative control.

Limits: every control is authored and was not run. On these bytes none can be reached, and the IC-2 adapter rows are never dispatched. These holds are disclosed. Nothing in this review proves runtime behaviour.

findings.fix:
In assertCommitted, assert the exact key sets of the readback envelope and its values against p:
- detail.publicReference equals the result;
- email equals lower(input email); name, phone and the two addresses equal the input;
- agreements equal the decision's required pairs;
- each line matches its snapshot;
- submittedEvent.occurredAt equals received_at;
- companion.intake equals the attribution snapshot;
- the obligation has the template key and payload;
- the outbox recipient equals p.notificationRecipient (query it directly).

findings.claim:
The commit has about 15 refusals for settings and identity. Only two have controls: a digest mismatch and lock_timeout set to 0. Deleting any of the others would go unnoticed:
- effective timer above the approved field;
- unsupported unit;
- catalog value differing from the effective statement, transaction or idle value;
- missing catalog timer binding;
- effective relationship, TimeZone or DateStyle mismatch;
- approved-limits identity mismatch, for example calling without 'set role service_role';
- zero, fractional or overflowing profile fields, and broken relationships between fields;
- an isolation level other than READ COMMITTED.

The final eligibility predicate has about 19 conditions. Controls cover member status, customer status, session revoked (also the row-11 concurrent revoke) and the EA actor mismatch. Removing any of these would go unnoticed:
- a member actor of the form member:<other uuid> using the same session context;
- configuredOwnerId differing from the session owner, or a different access_role;
- session expired, or issued in the future;
- input email differing from the customer's;
- a missing or wrongly versioned agreement acceptance, or input agreements without a required pair;
- legal verified_at or bound_at after the decision time.
An unknown session hash reaches 'select into strict', which raises an unmarked P0002 that the adapter treats as uncertain. That classification is NOT PROVEN to be intended.

The verifier never asserts:
- that receipt.currentnessDependency.status is SOURCE_ACCEPT_WITH_LIMITS;
- that its definitionSha256 equals receipt.currentnessDefinition;
- that the candidate's embedded expected_currentness_definition equals the receipt value (IC-12); it only checks that the value is not null;
- that the synthetic carrier's keys match bindingPacket.digestContract.fields;
- that receipt.disclosures.sharedBindings equals BINDINGS.
There is also no negative control showing that intake install or reapply refuses when currentness has drifted (IC-3).

verify.opinion:
Lens IB is accurate and well evidenced. I upheld all 15 findings at the lens's severity and stage: five P2 at S2 (IB-1 to IB-5) and ten P3. I made precision corrections to the fixes in IB-6, which suggests a price-ambiguous control that cannot be reached because the one-active index rules it out, and IB-13, where a decoy only discriminates if it carries the anchor-matching bytes. I challenged two statuses. Conflicting-payload refusal should be PARTIAL, not MET, because the post-lock conflict site is reached only through the race that is never forced to overlap. Deadline refusals should be PARTIAL, not NOT_MET, because the lock-timeout refusals are exercised and discriminate. Independent checks confirmed:
- every expected SQLSTATE, message and detail;
- the BINDINGS mapping (19 triggers, 55 inventory paths, schema version and review);
- that the empty-stock and quality-failed cases reach 'witness unavailable';
- that sessions are not a guarded relation, so the row-11 control does discriminate revoked_at.
I added six P3 items (IBV-1 to IBV-6). The most notable is that the IC-2 partial control cannot distinguish the pre-resolver check from the post-resolver one. In the verifier I found no fail-open save, no live enablement, no compiled operating value in a production path, no private-field leak and no fake receipt. The synthetic timers, attestations and construction receipt are labelled test fixtures inside a network-none disposable, and the verifier refuses to run on these bytes. Verdict for this lens: corrections required (P2 at S2), no P0 or P1. Every behaviour here is reasoned, NOT RUN.

upheld.claim:
Only the digest-mismatch and lock_timeout=0 refusals have controls. The following refusals could be deleted without detection:
- exceeds approved field;
- startup timer mismatch or missing binding;
- profile relationship, TimeZone or DateStyle;
- identity without set role;
- owner_id, access_role, expiry and issued-in-future;
- member actor of another uuid;
- email;
- agreement pairs;
- requiredRole pin;
- requestAcknowledged.
C-7 recovery is tested only with an invalid digest. This follows the doc 80 CS-12 precedent at P2.

The only behavioural control over buildDisabledQuickOrderProduction passes enteredAtMonotonicMsFor: () => null, so beginAttempt is never reached and the factory's own barriers are never exercised:
- intakeBudget with null values;
- maximumDelayMs 0;
- the throwing schedule;
- privateContextFor not being called.
The unmount proof rests on one regex over index.ts. production-deps.ts:138 writes a literal 'maximum' (doc 78 C-1). It is a refusal sentinel and not an operating value, so this is not P0. The factory supplies a real privateContextFor and clock, against C-11 'supplies nothing' and the adapter comments.

These fail closed by understating status, but they contradict the FINAL seal and the actual design:
- 'NON_FINAL / receipts PENDING' wording;
- PROVISIONALLY_* IC-7/IC-12;
- the 'reader candidate rollback' title and 'four service paths' text, with no intake-first order stated;
- the receipt note saying EA maps to private_early_access while the SQL uses member;
- C-6 described as a session-entry clock with no 'Node entry is not proxy arrival' disclaimer;
- an inaccurate acyclic sentence and seal sequence;
- regnamespace missing from the built-ins list;
- ACTIVE vs COMPLETE coordination state.

Problems:
- offer, readiness, estimate, quality and stock-changed refusals have no twins;
- the seal row uses an invented message;
- the commit('{}') case checks SQLSTATE only;
- currentnessDependency is accepted as any object;
- there is no starts_with decoy;
- IC2-partial passes with or without the pre-resolver check;
- TX05's wrong-version case checks no effects, replay or retry.

Problems:
- the outbox, receipt and same-key assertions are vacuous;
- there are no rows for the exact try-lock shape, beforeSend expiry, a synchronous throw after transmission, committed with persisted:false, or 55000/23505 without 'aborted';
- 'session origin' titles are stale;
- the root header says the stamp and context do not exist;
- the admin case is a generic negative.

Remaining gaps:
- the adapter mapping controls are never dispatched;
- there are no held-before-activation, window or epoch-change controls;
- the budget branches are effectively unreachable;
- the 600 ms timing margins are tight;
- nothing fails after the fifth write or at commit;
- there is no C-10 alternate-server control.
